p.src:
Try to repeat the one-dimensional mass construction without enlarging the $2\times2$ theory: $H=\boldsymbol{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf P+\beta m.$ Assume $\beta^2=\mathbb{1}$. The square contains the desired terms $\mathbf P^2+m^2$, but it also contains $m\sum_i\{\sigma_i,\beta\}P_i.$ To remove this term, one matrix $\beta$ would have to satisfy $\{\sigma_x,\beta\}
=
\{\sigma_y,\beta\}
=
\{\sigma_z,\beta\}
=0.$ No $2\times2$ matrix does so. The identity commutes with all three Pauli matrices, and every traceless $2\times2$ matrix is a linear combination of the Pauli matrices. Any such combination fails to anti-commute with the component parallel to it. The $2\times2$ algebra has no fourth independent matrix of the required kind.

